I. Introduction 
 
In addition to the 21 standard superferric dipole magnets of the Superconducting FRagment Separator (Super-FRS) of the Facility for Antiproton and Ion Research (FAIR) [1] located in Darmstadt, Germany, three special or branched superferric dipoles are needed to allow directing the beam towards two different directions (curved −9.75-or straight). Note that the 21 standard dipoles and the 3 branched ones are distributed in 8 dipole triplets as shown on Fig. 1 of [2]. Three of these triplets integrate one branched dipole which requirements are defined in Table I of [2]. 
